&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 150%;&quot;&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 150%;&quot;&gt;Compared to baseline (no brace), there is evidence, based on RCTs, that the use of unloader knee braces may lead to statistically significant improvements in pain (low certainty) and mobility&sol;function (moderate certainty using objective measures; low certainty using patient-reported measures); however, the effects of unloader knee braces on QoL were inconsistent (both no difference and statistically significant difference were found; very low certainty), and there was no difference in medication usage (moderate certainty). The single RCT that evaluated the effects of an unloader knee brace on disease progression was insufficiently powered. Based on NRSs, compared to baseline (no brace), there is evidence that the use of unloader knee braces may lead to statistically significant improvements in pain, mobility&sol;function using objective or patient-report outcome measures, and QoL, as well as a decrease in medication usage and likelihood of undergoing a TKA the longer the brace is worn (all very low certainty).&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 150%;&quot;&gt;Compared to a sham brace, there is evidence, based on a single RCT, that the use of an active unloader knee brace may lead to statistically significant greater improvements in pain, mobility&sol;function using patient-reported measures and QoL (all low certainty). No RCTs evaluating the effects of an active unloader knee brace on mobility&sol;function using objective measures, medication usage, or disease progression compared to sham were identified.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 150%;&quot;&gt;Compared to SOC, there is evidence, based on RCTs, that the use of unloader knee braces may lead to statistically significant greater improvements in pain and mobility&sol;function using patient-reported measures (both very low certainty); however, the differential effects of unloader knee braces compared to SOC on mobility&sol;function using objective measures were inconsistent (both no difference and statistically significant difference were found; very low certainty), and there was no difference in QoL or medication usage between the treatment groups. No RCTs evaluating the effects of an unloader knee brace on disease progression compared to SOC were identified. Based on NRSs, compared to SOC, there were no differential effects of unloader knee braces on pain, mobility&sol;function using objective or patient-report outcome measures, opioid use, or progression to TKA (all very low certainty). No NRSs evaluating the effects of an unloader knee brace on QoL compared to SOC were identified.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;text-decoration: underline;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Conclusion&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 150%;&quot;&gt;Unloader (valgus or varus) knee braces use adjustable straps, condylar pads, or pneumatic bladders to apply either a valgus or varus external force on the knee joint for the management of medial or lateral unicompartmental knee OA. Purported benefits of unloader knee braces include reduction of biomechanical forces on the degenerative compartment, realignment of the knee, and potential relief of pain and improvement in function.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 150%;&quot;&gt;Across study designs and comparators, the use of unloader knee braces leads to a consistent direction of effect on pain reduction (low to very low certainty) and mobility and&sol;or functional improvement (moderate to very low certainty) in individuals diagnosed with medial or lateral unicompartmental tibiofemoral knee OA. Importantly, no evidence was identified to support limiting unloader knee braces for individuals with a diagnosis of medial or lateral unicompartmental knee OA only if objective knee laxity is found on physical examination. The heterogeneity in the certainty of the evidence is dependent upon the study design (RCT vs. NRS), comparator [baseline (no brace) vs. SOC vs. sham], type of outcome measure used (objective vs. patient-reported), and duration of follow-up. Generally, the study limitations were similar across most of the analyzed publications and included small sample sizes, short durations of follow-up, missing data, lower compliance rates, and lack of blinding (participants and&sol;or assessors).&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 150%;&quot;&gt;Available secondary sources (i.e., systematic reviews, meta-analyses, clinical practice guidelines) evaluating the effects of unloader knee braces in individuals with a diagnosis of medial or lateral unicompartmental knee OA echoed the heterogeneity noted in this analysis and cited similar concerns related to the certainty of the available literature. Despite these issues, the authors of the identified meta-analysis (including more than 1 study) concluded that unloader knee braces improve pain and function, the authors of the identified systematic review concluded the majority of the reviewed literature supported the use of unloader knee braces for the treatment of pain, and finally, most of the evidence-based clinical practice guidelines identified (6 of 9) recommended &amp;#8216;for&amp;#8217; the use of unloader knee braces in the treatment&sol;management of pain or mobility&sol;functional limitations related to knee OA.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 150%;&quot;&gt;Based on a review of the currently available literature, there is sufficient evidence to support that the use of unloader knee braces may be a potential non-surgical therapeutic option for the reduction of pain or improvement in mobility and&sol;or function in some Medicare beneficiaries with a diagnosis of medial or lateral unicompartmental knee OA, regardless of the presence of objective joint laxity. Therefore, the criteria in the Knee Orthoses Local Coverage Determination will be expanded to allow knee orthoses with single or double upright, adjustable flexion and extension joint, medial-lateral and rotation control with valgus or varus adjustment when an ambulatory beneficiary&amp;#8217;s medical record includes a physical examination and imaging report that support the diagnosis of medial or lateral unicompartmental tibiofemoral OA, documentation of pain or mobility and&sol;or functional impairment secondary to medial or lateral unicompartmental knee OA, and documentation of the beneficiary&amp;#8217;s willingness to use the knee orthosis.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
